            ("p", "A predictive model is not automatically a scientific explanation. In neuroscience, this distinction matters: a network can decode a stimulus 
from calcium-imaging traces while relying on artifacts, correlated features, or a shortcut that has little to do with the biological computation we hoped to 
study."),
            ("h2", "Begin with the scientific question"),
            ("p", "Interpretability work should start before the architecture is chosen. I ask what claim the model is meant to support. Is it a decoder that 
can support a brain–computer interface? A hypothesis about which neural populations carry information? Or a tool for discovering latent structure in a large 
recording? Each goal imposes different validity requirements and suggests different explanations."),
            ("p", "For decoding studies, I favor models with explicit bottlenecks and well-defined inputs. Temporal convolutional networks are often a good 
baseline because their receptive fields can be inspected and their latency structure can be compared with known physiology. For circuit-level questions, 
graph-based representations are more natural when the biological object is relational."),
            ("h2", "Build a ladder of explanations"),
            ("p", "I use several complementary explanations rather than a single saliency map. First, global feature importance tells me whether the model is 
relying on plausible channels or time windows. Second, local attributions reveal how a prediction changes around individual trials. Third, ablation tests 
establish whether a claimed signal is necessary, not merely correlated. Finally, synthetic perturbations help identify failure modes that are invisible in a 
held-out set."),
            ("blockquote", "An explanation should survive the same scrutiny we apply to any other scientific measurement."),
            ("h2", "Validate against the biology"),
            ("p", "The most useful checks are often simple. Does the model's preferred temporal window match the stimulus onset? Are attributions stable across 
animals and sessions? Does performance collapse when a plausible nuisance variable is randomized? These tests connect the model back to the experimental design 
and make the resulting claims easier to falsify."),
            ("p", "None of this removes the need for careful experimental work. But it can turn a high-performing model into a sharper instrument for asking 
biological questions—and a more honest account of what the data actually support."),
        ],
    },
    {
        "slug": "machine-learning-single-cell-genomics",
        "date": "January 2025",
        "date_iso": "2025-01-21",
        "category": "Single-Cell",
        "read_time": "5 min read",
        "title": "Bridging Machine Learning and Single-Cell Genomics",
        "excerpt": "Both fields work with high-dimensional, noisy observations. The productive overlap is not a shared algorithm; it is a shared discipline of 
representation and validation.",
        "body": [
            ("p", "Single-cell genomics and machine learning are often described as an obvious pairing: large matrices in, biological insight out. The reality 
is more interesting. Much of the value comes from deciding what a representation should preserve, how batch effects should be separated from biology, and which 
forms of validation are credible."),
            ("h2", "Representation is a scientific choice"),
            ("p", "A cell-by-gene matrix is already a representation. Normalization, feature selection, and embedding choices determine what downstream 
clustering can discover. I try to make those choices explicit and to compare pipelines across several reasonable alternatives. A result that exists only under 
one normalization scheme is a fragile result, not a biological fact."),
            ("p", "Multi-modal data sharpen the problem. scRNA-Seq, ATAC-Seq, and spatial assays observe different layers of the same system. Integration 
methods can align these layers, but alignment is not the same as explanation. I prefer to evaluate whether an integrated representation recovers known cell 
types, preserves condition-specific signals, and remains stable across donors."),
            ("h2", "Use ML where it changes the question"),
            ("p", "Machine learning is most useful in this setting when it enables a question that would otherwise be impractical: latent-factor models for 
continuous cell states, graph methods for spatial neighborhoods, or Bayesian clustering for hierarchical cell-type structure. The point is not to add a neural 
network to a familiar pipeline, but to make a previously inaccessible measurement tractable."),
            ("p", "Validation should combine quantitative benchmarks with biological adjudication. Marker recovery, held-out donor performance, perturbation 
consistency, and spatial coherence each test a different part of the claim. A model that is excellent on one axis can be misleading on another."),
            ("h2", "Keep the pipeline inspectable"),
            ("p", "The best computational biology is reproducible. I favor workflow systems such as Nextflow or Snakemake, explicit versioning, and outputs that
can be inspected at each stage. Interpretability here is practical: a collaborator should be able to trace how a biological conclusion emerged from a raw count 
matrix."),
        ],
    },
    {
        "slug": "graph-neural-networks-connectomics",
        "date": "November 2024",
        "date_iso": "2024-11-08",
        "category": "Connectomics",
        "read_time": "7 min read",
        "title": "Scaling Graph Neural Networks for Connectomics",
        "excerpt": "Connectomic datasets are large, sparse, and biologically structured. Graph learning can expose synaptic motifs—but only if the pipeline 
respects the scale and uncertainty of the data.",
        "body": [
            ("p", "Connectomics asks us to reason about a network whose nodes and edges are only partially observed. Reconstructed neuron meshes carry 
segmentation uncertainty, synapse calls can be ambiguous, and the resulting graphs can contain millions of edges. Graph neural networks are a natural fit, but 
scaling them is as much a data-engineering problem as a modeling problem."),
            ("h2", "Treat the graph as a measurement"),
            ("p", "The first design decision is what counts as a node and an edge. A node might be a cell body, a reconstructed segment, or an abstract 
compartment; an edge might represent a synapse, a spatial contact, or an inferred connection. These choices change the learning problem. I prefer to preserve 
multiple relational views when possible and to evaluate models against biologically meaningful motifs rather than only node-level labels."),
            ("h2", "Respect sparsity and uncertainty"),
            ("p", "Dense matrix operations quickly become wasteful. Sparse message passing, neighbor sampling, and partitioned training make large connectomes 
tractable without discarding their structure. Equally important is uncertainty: an edge predicted with low confidence should not be treated like a verified 
synapse. Calibration and selective prediction are useful here because they let a downstream analyst decide where human review should focus."),
            ("p", "I also evaluate graph models under controlled graph perturbations. If a model's classification of synapse types changes dramatically after 
small, biologically plausible edits, the model may be learning from reconstruction artifacts rather than circuit structure."),
            ("h2", "Look for motifs, then test them"),
            ("p", "Graph embeddings can reveal candidate connectivity motifs, but candidate motifs are not conclusions. The next step is to compare them with 
null models that preserve degree distribution, spatial locality, or cell-type composition. That comparison is what turns a visually interesting cluster into a 
quantitative claim about circuit organization."),
            ("p", "Scaling the method is necessary but not sufficient. The goal is an inspectable, uncertainty-aware pipeline whose outputs can guide 
experiments—and whose failures are visible enough to learn from."),
